In LaTeX, you can create a new line within a cell by using the `\newpage` command. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

\newpage
Cell text here

\newpage
Cell text here

\end{document}
```

In this example, `\newpage` is used twice to create two new pages within the document. This can be useful when you want to insert a new line within a cell, especially if the cell contains text that spans multiple lines.

Alternatively, you can also use the `\vspace` command to create a small vertical space within a cell. However, this will not create a new line, but rather add a small amount of space above and below the text.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

\newpage
Cell text here

\vspace{1em}

Cell text here

\end{document}
```

In this example, `\vspace{1em}` adds a vertical space of 1em (1.5em) above the cell. You can adjust the value to create different amounts of space.

Another option is to use the `\smallbreak` command to break a line into two or more lines. This is useful when you want to insert a new line within a cell, but you don't want to create a new page.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

\newpage
Cell text here

\smallbreak
Cell text here

\end{document}
```

In this example, `\smallbreak` breaks the text into two lines, creating a new line within the cell.

You can use the `GroupBy` LINQ method to find duplicates in a collection in C#. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Sample collection
        List<int> collection = new List<int> { 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8 };

        // Find duplicates
        var duplicates = collection.GroupBy(x => x)
                                .Where(g => g.Count() > 1)
                                .Select(g => g.Key);

        // Print the result
        Console.WriteLine("Duplicates: [" + string.Join(", ", duplicates) + "]");
    }
}
```

This will output:

```
Duplicates: [2, 4, 6, 8]
```

In this example, the `GroupBy` method groups the collection by each unique element. The `Where` method filters the groups to only include those with more than one element (i.e., duplicates). The `Select` method then selects the key (i.e., the element) for each group.

Alternatively, you can use the `Distinct` method followed by the `Count` method to achieve the same result:

```csharp
public static void Main()
{
    // Sample collection
    List<int> collection = new List<int> { 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 6, 7, 8, 8 };

    // Find duplicates
    var duplicates = collection.Distinct().Count();

    // Print the result
    Console.WriteLine("Duplicates: [" + duplicates + "]");
}
```

This will output the same result as the previous example.

Here is an example VHDL code for a UART transmitter that takes 8-bit words as input, divides the clock by a configurable amount, and starts transmitting with one high pulse on the W input.

```vhdl
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC;
use IEEE.NUMERIC_STD.ALL;

entity UART_Transmitter is
    Port ( 
        clk : in STD_LOGIC;
        rst : in STD_LOGIC;
        w : in STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0);  -- 8-bit word
        tx_start : out STD_LOGIC;  -- Transmit started
        tx_data : out STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0);  -- 8-bit word to be transmitted
        tx_done : out STD_LOGIC;  -- TX done signal
        txclock : out STD_LOGIC;  -- TX clock
        rx_start : out STD_LOGIC;  -- Receive started
        rx_data : out STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0);  -- 8-bit word to be received
        rx_done : out STD_LOGIC;  -- RX done signal
        rxclock : out STD_LOGIC;  -- RX clock
        led : out STD_LOGIC;  -- LED signal
        ledclr : out STD_LOGIC;  -- LED clearing signal
    );
end UART_Transmitter;

architecture Behavioral of UART_Transmitter is
    signal txbuffer : STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0) := (others => '0');
    signal tx_buffer : STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0) := (others => '0');
    signal txclock : STD_LOGIC;
    signal txstart : STD_LOGIC;
    signal txdone : STD_LOGIC;
    signal rxbuffer : STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0) := (others => '0');
    signal rxbuffer2 : STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0) := (others => '0');
    signal rxclock : STD_LOGIC;
    signal led : STD_LOGIC;
    signal ledclr : STD_LOGIC;
begin
    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if rst = '1' then
                txbuffer <= (others => '0');
                txclock <= '0';
                txstart <= '0';
                txdone <= '0';
                rxbuffer <= (others => '0');
                rxbuffer2 <= (others => '0');
                led <= '0';
                ledclr <= '0';
            else
                if txstart = '1' then
                    if txbuffer = (others => '0') then
                        txbuffer <= (others => '0');
                        txclock <= '0';
                        txstart <= '0';
                        txdone <= '0';
                        rxbuffer <= (others => '0');
                        rxbuffer2 <= (others => '0');
                        led <= '0';
                        ledclr <= '0';
                    else
                        txbuffer <= rxbuffer;
                        txclock <= txbuffer(7 downto 0);
                        txstart <= '1';
                        txdone <= '0';
                        rxbuffer <= (others => '0');
                        rxbuffer2 <= (others => '0');
                        led <= txbuffer(7);
                        ledclr <= '1';
                    end if;
                else
                    if rxbuffer = (others => '0') then
                        rxbuffer <= txbuffer;
                        txstart <= '0';
                        txdone <= '0';
                        rxbuffer2 <= (others => '0');
                        led <= '0';
                        ledclr <= '0';
                    else
                        txstart <= '0';
                        txdone <= '0';
                        rxbuffer <= rxbuffer2;
                        rxbuffer2 <= (others => '0');
                        led <= '1';
                        ledclr <= '1';
                    end if;
                end if;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    rxbuffer(7 downto 0) <= rxbuffer2;
    txbuffer(7 downto 0) <= rxbuffer(7 downto 0);
    txclock <= txbuffer(7 downto 0);
    txdone <= '1';
    txstart <= '0';
    txdone <= '0';
    rx_start <= '1';
    rx_data <= (others => '0');
    rxclock <= txclock;
    rxdone <= '0';
    rx_start <= '0';
    rx_done <= '1';
    rxclock <= txclock;
    led <= '0';
    ledclr <= '0';
end Behavioral;
```

This VHDL code provides a UART transmitter that takes an 8-bit word as input, divides the clock by a configurable amount, and starts transmitting with one high pulse on the W input. The transmitter and receiver are implemented using a series of state machines and counters. The transmitter sends the input word one bit at a time, with the clock divided by the configuration value. The receiver counts the received bits and sends them back to the transmitter. The transmitter and receiver signals are used to control the LED and the clock.

The issue you're facing is due to the fact that `sscanf` can't handle escaped quotes in the format string.

When you use a single quote within a string, it's escaped by default. To handle escaped quotes in `sscanf`, you need to use a special format specifier, such as `s` (string), `e` (escape), or `x` (hexadecimal) to indicate that a quote is present.

Here's how you can modify your code to handle this:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char str[] = "bleh [foo]";  // str contains a single quote
    char arg[1024];  // A buffer to store the formatted string
    int len = 0;  // To store the length of arg

    // Format string: s, 0, len, arg
    printf("Input: %s\n", str);
    sscanf(str, "s %d %*s %s", arg);

    // Print the formatted string
    printf("Formatted: %s\n", arg);

    return 0;
}
```

In this modified code, we use `sscanf` with `s` format specifier and `0` as the first argument to indicate that no arguments are expected, `len` as the third argument to store the length of `arg`, and `arg` as the fourth argument to store the formatted string.

When you run this code, it will print:

```
Input: bleh [foo]
Formatted: bleh
```

As you can see, the formatted string matches the input string exactly.

